e league. Drake Maye has run the passing game, with DeMario Douglas leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Patriots defense has been about average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been sack rate, where it ranks 6th, and its weakest has been takeaways per game, where it ranks 19th. Elijah Ponder leads the team with 6 sacks, and Christian Barmore has added 3.5 despite playing only 50% of the snaps. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of pass rushers that ranks 6th highest in the league.","special":"The Patriots have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 77% of field goals, which is a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 19% of the money on the books. Their quarterbacks were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Drake Maye has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,983 yards. Targets have been spread around, with DeMario Douglas and Hunter Henry leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield has been a committee led by Rhamondre Stevenson and TreVeyon Henderson. On defense, Elijah Ponder leads the team with 6 sacks.","coaching":"Josh McDaniels is calling the offense and Zak Kuhr is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2024 Ravens under Todd Monken and the 2023 49ers under Kyle Shanahan. Their defense looks most like the 2024 and 2025 Steelers.","trends":"The offense went from 1st in EPA per play in 2025 to 8th so far this season, a modest decline. The defense moved from 11th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 16th so far this season, a modest decline.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}